WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.368 --> 00:00:03.169
-Hi, Demi. How are you?
-I'm good, Seth.

00:00:03.169 --> 00:00:06.806
-I like that we're both showing
off our beautiful stonework.

00:00:06.806 --> 00:00:08.374
[ Laughs ]
-Yes.

00:00:08.374 --> 00:00:10.042
-How have you been holding up?

00:00:10.042 --> 00:00:13.312
-I would say I feel so grateful

00:00:13.312 --> 00:00:16.649
that, all considered,
I'm holding up very well.

00:00:16.649 --> 00:00:20.887
I'm grateful
to be out of the bigger cities

00:00:20.887 --> 00:00:25.758
and out in small-town America
of Idaho,

00:00:25.758 --> 00:00:27.060
where I raised my kids.

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:31.197
And it's so beautiful
to look outside every day.

00:00:31.197 --> 00:00:33.366
So, yes.

00:00:33.366 --> 00:00:35.668
-Counting blessings. You --
I know you're spending the time

00:00:35.668 --> 00:00:37.137
with your three daughters.

00:00:37.137 --> 00:00:41.407
And how have they enjoyed
the extra time with you?

00:00:41.407 --> 00:00:43.510
I feel like parents
really appreciate it.

00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:45.511
I'm wondering how the kids feel.

00:00:45.511 --> 00:00:49.348
-I think that they have
equally appreciated --

00:00:49.348 --> 00:00:54.687
You know, they're -- They
kind of have come in and out,

00:00:54.687 --> 00:01:00.259
but a real testament
is their desire to get back here

00:01:00.259 --> 00:01:02.828
even when they've had to go back
to do some work,

00:01:02.828 --> 00:01:04.797
to all be together.

00:01:04.797 --> 00:01:08.601
And, you know,
that is really the gift

00:01:08.601 --> 00:01:10.636
in, you know, the challenge,

00:01:10.636 --> 00:01:14.707
is this quality time.

00:01:14.707 --> 00:01:17.610
And it's a great opportunity
to work on stuff,

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:19.679
because stuff is definitely
going to come up

00:01:19.679 --> 00:01:22.882
when you're all this close
together for so long.

00:01:22.882 --> 00:01:25.952
-That is very true.
But it strikes me that --

00:01:25.952 --> 00:01:27.853
I mean, I have a 2-year-old
and a 4-year-old,

00:01:27.853 --> 00:01:29.155
and I am so enjoying it.

00:01:29.155 --> 00:01:31.023
We also have our dog.
And our dog --

00:01:31.023 --> 00:01:35.027
I think dogs can, you know,
obviously sense your stress.

00:01:35.027 --> 00:01:37.563
And I think we're all
a little bit stressed out

00:01:37.563 --> 00:01:38.931
because of the time
we're living in.

00:01:38.931 --> 00:01:41.133
And so our dog has been acting
a little squirrelly.

00:01:41.133 --> 00:01:43.469
But you have how many dogs
in the house right now?

00:01:43.469 --> 00:01:44.604
Be honest, Demi.

00:01:44.604 --> 00:01:48.074
-Okay. So, I personally
have seven.

00:01:48.074 --> 00:01:52.145
Each of my daughters
have one or two pets.

00:01:55.648 --> 00:01:58.117
I have someone
who works with me,

00:01:58.117 --> 00:02:02.287
who's been working with me
for 27 years,

00:02:02.287 --> 00:02:04.123
who also brings a pet.

00:02:04.123 --> 00:02:07.026
My aunt is visiting.
She has a dog.

00:02:07.026 --> 00:02:08.361
I mean --

00:02:08.361 --> 00:02:11.164
-How do the dogs --
Are the dogs cohabiting well?

00:02:11.164 --> 00:02:12.699
Because I feel like
we're spending a lot of time

00:02:12.699 --> 00:02:15.335
talking about how family members
are getting through this time

00:02:15.335 --> 00:02:18.104
being under one roof.
How do the dogs handle it?

00:02:18.104 --> 00:02:22.208
-Look, I think for dogs in
general, their owners are home.

00:02:22.208 --> 00:02:25.912
They're getting more time
with them.

00:02:25.912 --> 00:02:29.415
You know, mine -- You know,
I have a lot of rescues,

00:02:29.415 --> 00:02:31.450
which, you know, I feel lucky

00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:34.387
that I can actually
take on this many.

00:02:34.387 --> 00:02:40.226
And in that grouping
is an entire litter of four.

00:02:40.226 --> 00:02:41.693
And so they're all --
-Oh, wow.

00:02:41.693 --> 00:02:44.997
-So, I have three.
My aunt has one.

00:02:44.997 --> 00:02:46.466
So they're loving it.

00:02:46.466 --> 00:02:49.269
They're, like, living
their best lives, truly.

00:02:49.269 --> 00:02:52.605
And, you know --
Oh, but a 2- and a 4-year-old.

00:02:52.605 --> 00:02:55.275
You guys are, like, on it.
-Yeah.

00:02:55.275 --> 00:02:57.510
-That's, like, full-on
entertainment.

00:02:57.510 --> 00:02:59.245
Like, you --
-It's good --

00:02:59.245 --> 00:03:01.280
Yeah, they definitely require
a lot of our attention.

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:04.650
But it's been really -- I mean,
especially with the 2-year-old,

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:06.585
you know, each day
feels like a lifetime,

00:03:06.585 --> 00:03:08.020
because they are just
a different person

00:03:08.020 --> 00:03:09.188
at the end of 24 hours.

00:03:09.188 --> 00:03:11.690
And because, you know,
ultimately

00:03:11.690 --> 00:03:13.293
they have so much more
of my attention,

00:03:13.293 --> 00:03:16.996
just because I'm working,
my hours are better,

00:03:16.996 --> 00:03:19.565
it's been really cool
to, like, watch them,

00:03:19.565 --> 00:03:20.933
I don't know, grow up.

00:03:20.933 --> 00:03:25.204
-Amazing. I mean, I really think
that is an incredible gift

00:03:25.204 --> 00:03:29.008
and something that I feel a lot
of people are gaining from this,

00:03:29.008 --> 00:03:32.011
which is things
that they can do at home

00:03:32.011 --> 00:03:33.646
that they didn't
realize they could.

00:03:33.646 --> 00:03:38.017
And I think, you know, wherever
this goes and whatever happens,

00:03:38.017 --> 00:03:42.454
I do think that there is going
to be some permanent change

00:03:42.454 --> 00:03:46.792
in our perception of what we do
and how we do it.

00:03:46.792 --> 00:03:48.461
-It's very true --
just in general,

00:03:48.461 --> 00:03:49.996
like, what is worth valuing

00:03:49.996 --> 00:03:52.765
and what is worth trying
to minimize in our lives.

00:03:52.765 --> 00:03:54.334
I want to ask this

00:03:54.334 --> 00:03:58.203
because you received,
I guess, some attention,

00:03:58.203 --> 00:04:00.206
is the right way
of putting this.

00:04:00.206 --> 00:04:04.944
Someone noted online
maybe some design touches

00:04:04.944 --> 00:04:08.681
that people would not have
expected from any human being.

00:04:08.681 --> 00:04:10.816
We're going to show the photo,

00:04:10.816 --> 00:04:14.420
but I think the thing
that was most jarring

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:17.556
was that you have carpeting
in your bathroom.

00:04:17.556 --> 00:04:20.727
-Okay. So --
Which is interesting,

00:04:20.727 --> 00:04:23.196
which actually, because
this is the house,

00:04:23.196 --> 00:04:25.531
again, that my children
grew up in,

00:04:25.531 --> 00:04:31.938
and that originally
was a Bruce Willis choice.

00:04:31.938 --> 00:04:33.005
-Okay.

00:04:33.005 --> 00:04:35.641
-So, not to put it off on him.

00:04:35.641 --> 00:04:38.610
We also live in the mountains
where it gets very cold.

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:43.415
So it's never bothered me.

00:04:43.415 --> 00:04:44.517
It's actually quite good.

00:04:44.517 --> 00:04:46.686
We put rugs in our bathroom.

00:04:46.686 --> 00:04:48.354
-Yep. That's true.

00:04:48.354 --> 00:04:51.257
-But I appreciate the interest

00:04:51.257 --> 00:04:56.128
that has gone into
all of my little oddities,

00:04:56.128 --> 00:04:59.766
because this, you know --
this place

00:04:59.766 --> 00:05:02.535
definitely houses
a lot of my treasures.

00:05:02.535 --> 00:05:05.438
And I have had some big laughs
over the comments

00:05:05.438 --> 00:05:07.840
with my family who know me well,

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:12.578
of all of my eccentricities
of small and large,

00:05:12.578 --> 00:05:16.115
you know, like
my giant tennis ball.

00:05:16.115 --> 00:05:18.217
-Right, a very helpful thing
to have around the house.

00:05:18.217 --> 00:05:19.485
-Everyone should have one.

00:05:19.485 --> 00:05:21.487
-[ Laughs ]
-I highly recommend.

00:05:21.487 --> 00:05:24.156
My giant safety pin.

00:05:24.156 --> 00:05:26.091
-Yep. Those are very --
They're great.

00:05:26.091 --> 00:05:28.527
-But let me --
I want to point out one thing.

00:05:28.527 --> 00:05:33.332
In all of the comments,

00:05:33.332 --> 00:05:36.369
I was most disappointed

00:05:36.369 --> 00:05:40.339
that nobody commented that
the couch that was in there,

00:05:40.339 --> 00:05:45.611
which I am sitting on right now,
is a miniature couch.

00:05:45.611 --> 00:05:46.945
-Oh, so, this is fascinating.

00:05:46.945 --> 00:05:48.748
I didn't realize you're actually
on the couch right now.

00:05:48.748 --> 00:05:51.717
So that means that behind --
Where I am right now

00:05:51.717 --> 00:05:54.086
is basically where the bathtub
is, correct?

00:05:54.086 --> 00:05:57.856
-Well, no, 'cause the couch
is not normally in my bathroom.

00:05:57.856 --> 00:05:59.491
-Oh, that makes me feel better,

00:05:59.491 --> 00:06:01.860
'cause the couch being there
also seemed weird.

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:03.629
-I had to move all that in

00:06:03.629 --> 00:06:07.233
because, you know,
we were doing this podcast

00:06:07.233 --> 00:06:10.136
that we're here to talk about,

00:06:10.136 --> 00:06:14.473
and we had to find the place
for the best sound.

00:06:14.473 --> 00:06:16.642
-Yes.
-So, I did a test in my closet.

00:06:16.642 --> 00:06:18.944
I did in various places.

00:06:18.944 --> 00:06:21.914
What came back is that
the bathroom had the best sound.

00:06:21.914 --> 00:06:25.117
So, I was in there
for many hours a day.

00:06:25.117 --> 00:06:26.185
But I didn't want to --

00:06:26.185 --> 00:06:28.787
I couldn't bring
full-size furniture in.

00:06:28.787 --> 00:06:30.555
So, this little miniature couch

00:06:30.555 --> 00:06:34.427
was something that I had made
for my children long ago.

00:06:34.427 --> 00:06:37.096
And so I just whipped this baby
in there.

00:06:37.096 --> 00:06:39.398
-That's fantastic. So, giant
tennis balls, tiny couches --

00:06:39.398 --> 00:06:41.233
That's basically
how you're living your life.

00:06:41.233 --> 00:06:42.235
-There you go.

